"Uptown Memorial is a mid-sized hospital a block east of the Lawrence L stop, in Uptown."
"From the Lawrence platform it is a stair down, then one snowy block east to the ER doors."
"The ER waiting room is warm and bright, with rows of chairs, a muted TV and a triage window."
"At half past five on a Saturday it is quiet: a few people waiting, one man asleep, a cough."
"A guard sits by the sliding doors beside a walk-through metal detector."
"Triage asks name, birth date and address; a patient may give none and still be seen."
"A patient with no ID is logged under a placeholder name until she gives one."
"Staff must report abuse of children and elders, but not of an adult who does not ask."
"Police come only if a patient asks for them or a crime is reported."
"The social worker comes on at eight; overnight the charge nurse calls one on call."
"The hospital keeps a closet of donated coats, shoes, sweatpants and underwear."
"Heroes and Avowed injuries go to the House of Healing, not here; this ER sees ordinary cases."
